      "writtenOn": "2026-08-17T22:58:21Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Critical performance issue: Unbounded Git commit graph traversal to the repository root.\n\nWhen `thisPsd` is the bottom-most change in a relation chain (or any change whose parent is already merged into the target branch like `master`), `initialParent` is not present in `byId` because `byId` only contains patch sets of the changes returned by the query.\n\nSince no commit in `master`\u0027s ancestry will exist in `byId`, `psd !\u003d null` will never evaluate to true. As a result, this BFS while loop will traverse the entire Git commit graph all the way back to the root commit(s) of the repository, parsing headers on every single commit in repository history.\n\nIn large repositories (e.g. Gerrit with ~50k commits, Chromium with \u003e1.5M commits), this turns an in-memory lookup into a catastrophic disk I/O and object parsing operation on every GET /related request. Furthermore, because `findParentsInGraph` is called unconditionally in `sort()` and `sortAncestors()`, this traversal occurs on every request regardless of whether `EXCLUDE_ABANDONED` was supplied.\n\nWe need a stopping condition or boundary check so we do not traverse commits beyond the known scope of the relation chain.",
      "isAi": true,
      "revId": "4cdb7bd065417323aeae5eb6c7b8d0124ee400ee",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
